From 3a515a79adcd001f158290b4fb88b00a61a4eca0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:07:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/34] input: repeat the current item in place

Repeating one item is currently the playlist's job: the input reaches the
end and dies, NextItem() hands the same item straight back, and
PlayItem() builds a new input for it. Everything underneath goes with it
-- the demuxer and decoder are reopened, and aout_DecDelete() stops the
audio output, so the sink's stream is destroyed and recreated on every
pass. On a sink with real latency that is audible: over Bluetooth each
loop logs "cannot synchronize start", defers the start twice and then
starts late, which is a break at the loop point and leaves the drift
correction chasing an offset that was never a drift.

MainLoopTryRepeat() already knows how to do this without any of it, by
seeking back to the start, which is what --input-repeat uses. Consult the
playlist's "repeat" setting when no explicit repeat count was given.

Only for inputs that can be rewound: the media library and the preparser
run inputs whose demuxers cannot seek, and a playlist setting has no
business reaching them. The setting is read at each end of stream rather
than latched when the input starts, so toggling repeat during playback
applies from the end of the current pass and clearing it lets the input
finish and hand back to the playlist as before.

Note that a pass no longer starts a new input, so per-item events such as
the played counter fire once for the whole run rather than once per loop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/input/input.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/input/input.c b/src/input/input.c
index 35a8c35fb7..5c3b7c1f33 100644
--- a/src/input/input.c
+++ b/src/input/input.c
@@ -614,9 +614,21 @@ static int MainLoopTryRepeat( input_thread_t *p_input )
 {
     input_thread_private_t *priv = input_priv(p_input);
     int i_repeat = var_GetInteger( p_input, "input-repeat" );
-    if( i_repeat <= 0 )
+
+    if( i_repeat < 0 )
         return VLC_EGENERIC;
 
+    if( i_repeat == 0 )
+    {
+        /* The playlist would otherwise honour this by destroying the input
+         * and building a new one, which takes the demuxer, decoder and audio
+         * output down and back up at every loop. Only for inputs that can be
+         * rewound and belong to the playlist. */
+        if( !var_GetBool( p_input, "can-seek" )
+         || !var_InheritBool( p_input, "repeat" ) )
+            return VLC_EGENERIC;
+    }
+
     /* A repeat is a seek back to the start, and a seek that does not take
      * leaves the end of stream exactly as it was, so the next pass through the
      * main loop asks for the same repeat again. Nothing bounded that beyond
